THIRD-PARTY SOURCE IN THE REPOSITORY

exit-daemon/third_party/onionmasq holds three crates of Onionmasq (Tor Project, commit
849a8d14d165), licensed MIT OR Apache-2.0; their licence texts are copied unchanged
(LICENSE-MIT, LICENSE-APACHE) and the changes are the patches in
exit-daemon/third_party/patches (see exit-daemon/SOURCE.md).

exit-daemon/Cargo.lock names 548 Rust packages from crates.io and 4 in-tree packages (the daemon
and the three Onionmasq crates). The 548 are fetched at build time; the ones a target needs
are linked statically into the `exitd` binary (Arti, aws-lc and others, with a mix of MIT,
Apache-2.0, ISC, OpenSSL-style, MPL-2.0 and similar licences). exit-daemon/NOTICE lists the
licence expression of every package. The lock lists every package that any target may
resolve; the subset linked into `exitd` has not been computed. The aggregated attribution
that a shipped binary needs (for example from `cargo about`) is not generated yet; it must
exist, and `cargo audit` must have been run again (RISKS.md names the run of 2026-10-22),
before an image carries the daemon.

vault-core/Cargo.lock names 72 Rust packages from crates.io and the crate itself; each is
pinned with its SHA-256 and listed with its licence in vault-core/SOURCE.md and in the SBOM
of oblivios-docs. The crate psl compiles in the Public Suffix List (data/rules.txt of psl
2.1.244), which is MPL-2.0 (publicsuffix.org); the list is not modified by this project.

